 # MazeParse::ragged
 
 `MazeParse::ragged` is issued when `MazeParse` reads a `.maze` file in which some
-line is shorter than the header row, so the character grid is not rectangular.
-This usually indicates trailing characters stripped by an editor or a truncated
-file; the maze still imports, but the short rows are completed with wall cells.
+line is shorter or longer than the header row, so the character grid is not rectangular.
+This usually indicates trailing characters stripped by an editor, a truncated
+file, or stray characters appended to a line; the maze still imports, but every
+row is fitted to the width of the header row.
 
-- The ragged line is padded on the right with `"#"` (wall) characters up to the width of the header row, so the result is always a full rectangular matrix.
-- The message is issued once per import, even when several lines are ragged; the reported line number is that of the shortest line.
+- A shorter line is padded on the right with `"#"` (wall) characters up to the width of the header row; a longer line is truncated to that width, dropping its extra characters. The result is always a full rectangular matrix as wide as the header row.
+- The message is issued once per import, even when several lines are ragged; it reports the first line whose length differs from the header row's, whether it is shorter or longer, and both lengths.
 - Use [Quiet]() or `Off[MazeParse::ragged]` to suppress the message when ragged files are expected.
 
